    "points": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "heading": "Compound tenses in depth",
        "body": "A compound tense uses two parts: an auxiliary verb (usually forms of haber) plus a past participle (often ending in -ado / -ido). The participle stays the same; the auxiliary changes for the person.\n\nExamples: he cantado (I have sung), había comido (I had eaten), habrá vivido (will have lived). Think: helper verb + done-form of the main verb.\n\nPráctica: Write five compound forms with the same participle (cantado or comido) using he / había / hube / habré / habría. Circle the auxiliary and underline the participle.\n\nError común: Changing the participle by person (hemos comidos). The participle does not take plural -s here."
      }
    ],
    "summary": ""
  },
